      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE",
          "content": "An association between aortic arch atherosclerosis and vascular events has been demonstrated. However, few data exist regarding follow-up evaluation of this disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In this study, 183 patients with the diagnosis of aortic arch atherosclerosis were prospectively followed up. This diagnosis was made during an echocardiographic cross-sectional study. In 136 patients, raised plaques with thickness < 5 mm had been shown to exist, and in 47 patients complex plaques with thickness > or = 5 mm or plaques with mobile components had been demonstrated on the initial transesophageal echocardiography."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During a mean follow-up period of 16 +/- 7 months, vascular events with a presumed embolic origin occurred in 15 patients. The incidence was 4.1 per 100 person-years in patients with raised plaques compared with 13.7 per 100 person-years in the group with complex plaques. The Kaplan-Meier survival analysis revealed a significantly higher rate of vascular events in patients who were found to have complex plaques (P < .01). In the Cox proportional hazards analysis, the finding of complex plaques (relative risk [RR], 4.3; 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.5 to 12.0; P = .006), coronary artery disease (RR, 4.0; 95% CI, 1.2 to 13.1; P = .02), and a history of previous embolism (RR, 4.0; 95% CI, 1.1 to 14.4; P = .03) were independent predictors of vascular events."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients with the finding of protruding plaques or plaques with mobile components have a high risk of subsequent vascular events."
        }
      ]},
